            <div class="path-panels">

                {# ---- PATH 1: Your Mac/Linux device ---- #}
                <div class="path-panel" id="panel-local" role="region" aria-labelledby="path-local">
                    <h3>Run it on your Mac or Linux device</h3>
                    <p>Perfect for kicking the tires. Free, runs in a minute. The catch: it sleeps when your machine sleeps, so your phone and your AIs can't reach it while it's closed. You'll need <a href="https://bun.sh" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bun</a> 1.3 or later, then one command:</p>

                    <div class="code-inline">
                        <code>bun add -g @openparachute/hub &amp;&amp; parachute init</code>
                    </div>

                    <p><code>parachute init</code> starts your hub, installs your vault &mdash; the place all your notes live &mdash; and opens a browser setup wizard: <strong>Account &rarr; Vault &rarr; Expose &rarr; Done</strong>. On a laptop the Expose step auto-skips; you're signed in locally.</p>

                    <details>
                        <summary>On Bun 1.2.x / checking your version</summary>
                        <ul style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                            <li><strong>Check it.</strong> <code>bun --version</code> &mdash; if it's below 1.3, run <code>bun upgrade</code>.</li>
                            <li><strong>Stuck on 1.2.x?</strong> You may see a cosmetic <code>InvalidPackageResolution</code> or <code>FileNotFound</code> warning during install &mdash; the package installs anyway. Upgrading to Bun 1.3 clears it.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </details>

                    <details>
                        <summary>Ports, exposing it, tokens, and re-running</summary>
                        <ul style="margin-top: 1rem;">
                            <li><strong>Ports.</strong> The hub answers on <code>1939</code>; the vault runs on <code>1940</code> behind it.</li>
                            <li><strong>Going beyond your laptop.</strong> <code>init</code> offers to expose your hub publicly (a Cloudflare Tunnel is the default on a server &mdash; it'll offer to install <code>cloudflared</code> for you on macOS and Linux). If an expose attempt fails, <code>init</code> keeps going on the local URL and prints a retry hint: <code>parachute expose public --cloudflare</code>.</li>
                            <li><strong>Bootstrap token.</strong> When the hub is exposed publicly and no admin exists yet, <code>init</code> prints a one-time bootstrap token; the wizard asks for it before it lets you create the first admin (local-only installs skip this).</li>
                            <li><strong>Safe to re-run.</strong> <code>init</code> is idempotent. Check the stack any time with <code>parachute status</code>.</li>
                        </ul>
                    </details>

                    <p style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">Want it reachable from your phone and your AIs around the clock? Move to an <a href="#" class="switch-to-do">always-on DigitalOcean box</a> anytime &mdash; same stack, nothing's locked in.</p>
                </div>

                {# ---- PATH 2: DigitalOcean (default) ---- #}
                <div class="path-panel" id="panel-do" role="region" aria-labelledby="path-do">
                    <h3>Stand up an always-on home on DigitalOcean</h3>
                    <p>The <strong>zero-SSH</strong>, Render-like way to a real always-on Parachute: create a droplet, paste <strong>one block</strong> (no credentials in it), wait a couple of minutes, then copy a <strong>one-time bootstrap token</strong> from the droplet Console and set your username + password <strong>in your browser</strong>. <strong>You never SSH in.</strong> The droplet self-installs hub + vault and gets a real Let's Encrypt cert via <a href="https://caddyserver.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Caddy</a> on first boot.</p>

                    <h4 class="path-step">Create the droplet</h4>
                    <p>In the DigitalOcean console (<a href="https://cloud.digitalocean.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">cloud.digitalocean.com</a>) &rarr; <strong>Create &rarr; Droplets</strong>:</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Image:</strong> Ubuntu 24.04 (LTS) x64.</li>
                        <li><strong>Size:</strong> $6/mo (1 GB) runs hub + vault. Pick $12/mo (2 GB) for on-box transcription (Scribe) or to run Claude Code on the box.</li>
                        <li><strong>Region:</strong> nearest you.</li>
                        <li>Expand <strong>Advanced options &rarr; Add Initialization scripts (user data)</strong> and paste the block below as-is (optionally uncomment the domain line):</li>
                    </ul>

                    <div class="code-inline" style="text-align:left;">
                        <code>#!/bin/bash<br>
                        <span class="muted"># export PARACHUTE_DOMAIN="vault.example.com"&nbsp;&nbsp;# optional</span><br>
                        <span class="muted"># export PARACHUTE_CHANNEL="rc"&nbsp;&nbsp;# optional &mdash; track release candidates</span><br>
                        curl -fsSL https://parachute.computer/install/digitalocean.sh | bash</code>
                    </div>

                    <p class="muted" style="font-size:0.9em;"><strong>Why no password here?</strong> Anything in User Data persists in plain text &mdash; readable on the box, in the droplet console, and via the metadata service. So no username or password goes in here. Instead the hub mints a one-time bootstrap token on first boot, and you set your own password in the browser. Your password never touches the cloud config.</p>

                    <p>Click <strong>Create</strong>. cloud-init runs that script as root early in first boot.</p>

                    <h4 class="path-step">Wait ~2&ndash;3 minutes, then finish in your browser</h4>
                    <p>Give the droplet a couple of minutes after it shows <strong>Active</strong> &mdash; cloud-init installs everything and Caddy fetches the TLS cert on the first HTTPS hit. Then, with <strong>no SSH key needed</strong>:</p>
                    <ol>
                        <li><strong>Open the droplet Console.</strong> On your droplet's page click <strong>Access &rarr; Launch Droplet Console</strong> for a browser root shell &mdash; no SSH key required.</li>
                        <li><strong>Read the setup file.</strong> In that console run <code>cat parachute-setup.txt</code>. Near the top it prints your <strong>bootstrap token</strong> (<code>parachute-bootstrap-&hellip;</code>) and your <strong>setup URL</strong> (<code>https://&lt;your-host&gt;/admin/setup</code>). Copy both.</li>
                        <li><strong>Open the setup URL in your browser</strong> &mdash; with a domain, <code>https://&lt;your-domain&gt;/admin/setup</code>; without one, <code>https://&lt;droplet-ip&gt;.sslip.io/admin/setup</code> (substitute your droplet's public IPv4, dots and all, e.g. <code>https://203.0.113.5.sslip.io/admin/setup</code>).</li>
                        <li><strong>Paste the bootstrap token, then choose your own username + password.</strong> That's it &mdash; you're the owner, and your password was set in the browser, never stored in the cloud config.</li>
                    </ol>

                    <p class="muted" style="font-size:0.9em;">The token is single-use and regenerates if the hub restarts. Re-read the current one from the droplet Console any time with <code>cat parachute-setup.txt</code>.</p>

                    <p style="margin-top: 1.25rem;"><a href="/deploy/digitalocean/">Advanced options &amp; troubleshooting &rarr;</a> &mdash; the SSH path (watch it install line by line), driving it from <code>doctl</code>, bringing your own domain, alternative ingress, and pitfalls.</p>
                </div>

                {# ---- PATH 3: Advanced / other ---- #}
                <div class="path-panel" id="panel-advanced" role="region" aria-labelledby="path-advanced">
                    <h3>Advanced / other</h3>
                    <p>The same bun-native flow works anywhere you have a box. Reach for these when you want something other than the happy path above.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><strong>Any other VPS.</strong> Hetzner, EC2, Linode, or a box in your closet &mdash; same <code>bun add -g @openparachute/hub &amp;&amp; parachute init</code> flow. On an SSH'd server <code>init</code> pre-selects public exposure so the wizard URL is reachable. See <a href="/deploy/vps/">Deploy to any VPS</a>.</li>
                        <li><strong>Bring your own domain.</strong> Point an A record at the box and set <code>PARACHUTE_DOMAIN="vault.example.com"</code> &mdash; Caddy certs your domain instead of the zero-config <code>&lt;ip&gt;.sslip.io</code> host. Sturdier for anything long-lived.</li>
                        <li><strong>Alternative ingress.</strong> Prefer not to open ports 80/443, or want a private tailnet-only hub? A <strong>Cloudflare Tunnel</strong> (<code>parachute expose public --cloudflare --domain vault.example.com</code>) or <strong>Tailscale</strong> (<code>parachute expose public</code> &rarr; a <code>*.ts.net</code> URL) both work as alternatives to Caddy-direct.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">Full walkthroughs, the SSH path, cloud-init internals, and pitfalls live in the deep guide: <a href="/deploy/digitalocean/">Deploy to DigitalOcean &rarr;</a> (the same flow applies to any VPS).</p>
                </div>

            </div>
        </fieldset>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>2 &middot; Finish the wizard</h2>

        <p style="margin-bottom: 1.25rem;">
            <span class="status-badge status-done">Account</span> <span style="color: var(--fg-dim);">&rarr;</span>
            <span class="status-badge status-done">Vault</span> <span style="color: var(--fg-dim);">&rarr;</span>
            <span class="status-badge status-done">Expose</span> <span style="color: var(--fg-dim);">&rarr;</span>
            <span class="status-badge status-done">Done</span>
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li><strong>Account.</strong> Create your admin login (on DigitalOcean this is the browser <code>/admin/setup</code> step above &mdash; paste the bootstrap token, choose your username + password).</li>
            <li><strong>Vault.</strong> Create your first vault &mdash; or import one from a git repo, or skip for now.</li>
            <li><strong>Expose.</strong> Choose how (or whether) to reach your hub from outside. This step <em>auto-skips on a local laptop</em>, and <em>pre-selects public on an SSH'd server</em> so the wizard URL is reachable.</li>
            <li><strong>Done.</strong> The wizard hands you everything you need to connect your AI.</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="connect-mcp-clients">
        <h2>3 &middot; Connect your AI</h2>

        <p>Connecting any AI &mdash; Claude.ai on the web, ChatGPT, Claude Code, Cursor, Zed, your own agent &mdash; comes down to one URL: your vault's MCP endpoint. Point your client at it (web UIs call this a <strong>connector</strong>):</p>

        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>&lt;hub-origin&gt;/vault/&lt;name&gt;/mcp</code>
        </div>

        <p><code>&lt;hub-origin&gt;</code> is the address your hub answers on &mdash; the same URL you sign in at, <em>not</em> the vault's own port <code>1940</code>. <code>&lt;name&gt;</code> is your vault name (<code>default</code> unless you changed it). The <strong><code>/mcp</code> suffix is required</strong> &mdash; the bare path is a metadata page, not the endpoint. OAuth-capable clients send you through hub sign-in automatically.</p>

        <p>Pick your client:</p>

        <details open>
            <summary>Claude.ai, ChatGPT &amp; other web AIs</summary>
            <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">In your AI's settings, add a new <strong>connector</strong> (custom MCP server) pointing at <code>&lt;hub-origin&gt;/vault/&lt;name&gt;/mcp</code>. You'll be sent to your hub to sign in and approve &mdash; then the AI can read and write your vault. Nothing to install; the easiest path for most people.</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Claude Code &amp; other CLI / MCP clients</summary>
            <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">Register the server once with <code>claude mcp add</code> &mdash; the wizard's done screen gives you a ready-to-paste command. The first time it connects, it sends you through your hub's sign-in in the browser (OAuth); after that, start a new session (MCP config loads at session start) and run <code>/mcp</code> &mdash; the vault's tools are there. Codex, Goose, Cursor, and Zed take the same <code>&lt;hub-origin&gt;/vault/&lt;name&gt;/mcp</code> URL. (Headless box that can't do the browser flow? Mint a token on the vault's Tokens page and append <code>--header "Authorization: Bearer &lt;token&gt;"</code>.)</p>
        </details>

        <details>
            <summary>Clients that need a bearer token</summary>
            <p style="margin-top: 1rem;">Some clients can't do OAuth and want a static token instead. Mint one from the vault admin SPA's <strong>Tokens</strong> page, then give it to the client alongside the same <code>&lt;hub-origin&gt;/vault/&lt;name&gt;/mcp</code> URL.</p>
        </details>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
        <h2>&#9733; 4 &middot; See your first note</h2>

        <p>Here's the payoff. Ask your AI to jot a quick note (&ldquo;jot down that I'm trying Parachute today&rdquo;), then open it in a browser &mdash; two ways:</p>

        <p><strong>Fastest &mdash; nothing to install.</strong> Open <a href="https://notes.parachute.computer" target="_blank">notes.parachute.computer</a>, the hosted Notes app. Enter your hub's vault URL (<code>&lt;hub-origin&gt;/vault/&lt;name&gt;</code>) and sign in. The app runs in your browser; your notes never leave your hub. There's the note you just made.</p>

        <p><strong>Host it on your own hub.</strong> Install <strong>Surface</strong> &mdash; the module that serves your UIs from your own hub &mdash; and it brings up Notes automatically:</p>

        <div class="code-inline">
            <code>parachute install surface</code>
        </div>

        <p>Open <a href="http://localhost:1939/surface/notes/" target="_blank"><code>localhost:1939/surface/notes/</code></a>, click the vault popover, and hit <strong>Connect</strong>. The same Notes, now served from your own hub &mdash; plus search, tag views, a visual map of how your notes link together, voice capture, and a markdown editor. One install covers every vault on your hub. Notes is the first surface; you can also build your own (below).</p>

        <div class="starter-callout">
            <h3>Two prompts, paste-and-go &mdash; fill your vault, then shape your view</h3>
            <p><strong>1. Fill your vault.</strong> Your vault starts mostly empty. This prompt interviews you about how you think, proposes a tag and folder structure, and imports what you already have &mdash; built on your say-so.</p>
            <a href="/onboarding/vault-setup/" class="button">Open the fill-your-vault prompt &rarr;</a>
            <p style="margin-top: 1.5rem;"><strong>2. Shape your view.</strong> This prompt interviews you, then builds a custom UI over your vault &mdash; a static SPA on the surface SDK, hosted on your own hub or GitHub Pages.</p>
            <a href="/onboarding/surface-build/" class="button">Open the shape-your-view prompt &rarr;</a>
            <p class="starter-adjacent">Do them in order: structure what's in the vault first, then build how you see it. (Power users: you can also <a href="/scripting/">script your vault</a> from bash, Python, or JS for imports, cron jobs, and CI.)</p>
        </div>
    </section>

